     "text": "Most adults who learn English as a second language find one or two sounds really hard. For French speakers, it’s usually “th” and “h”. The reason is simple: your mouth has never needed them, so it uses the closest sounds it knows.\n\nThe fastest way to fix this is not listening for hours. It’s short, daily practice with your mouth. Stand in front of a mirror for five minutes. Say “think, thanks, three, Thursday” slowly, and check that you can see the tip of your tongue each time. Then say “this, that, the, mother” with the same tongue position, but add your voice.\n\nFor the h, hold your hand in front of your mouth. On “happy” and “hungry”, you should feel a little warm air. On “hour” and “honest”, you shouldn’t.\n\nAfter a week, try it in real life: say “thanks” to every cashier, on purpose. It feels strange at first, but small daily practice is how new sounds become automatic.",
